## Lore

Loaks were made, not born. Some thirty or forty years ago a group of
Gene-Splicers took an aquatic D-Bee race and rebuilt it into a hunter of
mages, psychics and supernatural beings; why, nobody knows. A Loak is tall
and fish-headed, with translucent white or gray skin over a cartilage
skeleton, a shark''s mouth, round dark eyes, a fin down the spine, gills in
the neck and long webbed fingers ending in dagger-like claws.

They live and travel by water, which suits them well: the rivers and lakes
of Minnesota, Wisconsin, Michigan, the Ohio Valley and southern Ontario are
thick with mages, psychics and monsters. A Loak smells psychic and magical
energy the way a bloodhound smells a trail, hides inside a psionic void of
its own, feeds on the life energy of the prey it kills and can grow a suit
of living armor from ectoplasm. They reproduce by cloning themselves in
cocoons, and their numbers are climbing fast.

Most are instinctive killers with the manners of a tamed tiger. Some have
learned to live among people as bounty hunters, lawmen, demon slayers and
town protectors who turn their instincts only on monsters and villains.

## GM Notes

The book heads the race an optional player character and NPC. Also called
Mage Slayers, Mystic Hunters and Mage Assassins.

Feral Loaks make excellent NPC monsters, hunting good and evil alike. The
Coalition Army quietly lets Loaks escape as long as they are killing the
CS''s enemies.

Size 6 to 8 feet (1.8 to 2.4 m). Weight 200 to 320 pounds (90 to 144 kg).
Life span estimated at 6D6+110 years; they do not seem to age. Loaks have no
sexes and reproduce only by spawning.

Allies: humans and D-Bees without psychic or magic abilities; hunters,
warriors, Men-at-Arms and Wilderness Scouts. Enemies: all psychics (favorite
prey Psi-Stalkers, Crazies, Cyber-Knights, Bursters, Zappers, Mind Bleeders,
Mind Melters, Mystics, Psi-Goblins and Dragon-Cats); all practitioners of
magic; creatures of magic (dragons, sphinxes, Witchlings and Black Faeries
first); and every kind of supernatural being, evil ones first.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'loak');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'loak';

